# Thali App — Non-Indian / International Recipe Dataset (~111 dishes)

## TL;DR
- **Weight the dataset heavily toward Indo-Chinese and Italian/fast-food.** Per Swiggy's "How India Swiggy'd 2025" report (Jan 1–Nov 30, 2025), biryani was #1 with 93 million orders (10th straight year), burgers #2 at 44.2 million, and pizzas #3 at 40.1 million — so this set is **22 Indo-Chinese, 15 Italian, 20 Continental/American**, with Mexican, Thai, Middle Eastern, East Asian, desserts and beverages filling out **111 dishes total**.
- **Fasting flags (ekadashiSafe, navratriSafe, jainSafe) are "No" for essentially every dish here** — these are grain/onion/garlic/root-vegetable-based foods, exactly what Hindu/Jain fasting excludes. That is expected and correct; the app simply won't offer them on fast days.
- **The flags that matter — vegan, glutenFree, lactoseFree, diabeticFriendly, onionGarlicFree — are classified conservatively.** Refined-flour (maida) bases, white rice, deep-fried items and sugary desserts are marked "No" or "Partial" for diabeticFriendly, never overstated, because the app serves families with Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes.

## Key Findings
1. **Indo-Chinese is the dominant non-Indian cuisine in India.** Chinese cuisine makes up 33% of all restaurant types in India, per GlobalData's Online Menu Intelligence analysis of 12 million menu items (H1 2024) — the single largest cuisine footprint, with chicken the most-used ingredient (22% of items). Noodle bowls ranked #3 nationally on Zomato in 2023 with over 4.55 crore (45.5M) orders, and momos, manchurian, fried rice and spring rolls all sustain very high volumes. **Weight it heaviest.**
2. **Italian (pizza + pasta) is the second pillar.** Pizza was #3 on Swiggy 2025 (40.1M orders) and #2 on Zomato 2023 (7.45 crore / 74.5M orders), per each platform's year-end report. Pasta is a major cafe category — per Swiggy's 2024 report, "A single consumer in Bengaluru spent ₹49,900 on a pasta extravaganza, devouring almost 55 Alfredo dishes, 40 Mac & Cheese, and 30 Spaghetti plates."
3. **Fast food (burgers, fries, rolls, sandwiches) is huge.** Burgers are now #2 nationally on Swiggy (44.2M in 2025); chicken rolls and fries top the snack charts.
4. **Korean, Mexican and Middle Eastern are the fast-rising "emerging" cuisines.** Swiggy reported a 50% year-on-year increase in Korean food orders (with Gen Z driving 27% of them); Swiggy 2025 logged Mexican at 16 million orders, Tibetan at 12 million+, and Korean at 4.7 million; and Hyderabad "ordered the most chicken shawarmas" nationally per Swiggy 2024. Include a meaningful but smaller allocation for these.
5. **Strong vegetarian representation is essential** because a large share of Indian families are vegetarian; every cuisine block below leads with veg options and flags veg/non-veg clearly.
6. **Diabetic-friendliness must be conservative.** White rice has a high glycemic index — Harvard Health classifies "GI of 70 or higher" as high and notes "A serving of white rice has almost the same effect as eating pure glucose"; a study in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition found rice GI ranged from 64±9 to 93±11. Refined-flour pasta/pizza/noodles/bread sit in the moderate-high range and deep-fried/sugary items spike glucose — so these are marked No/Partial.

## Details

### How to read the tables
- **Type:** V = vegetarian, NV = non-vegetarian (contains meat/fish/egg).
- **Role:** the app plate-role (dal / rice / bread / sabzi / accompaniment / beverage / snack / dessert / main).
- **Flags:** Y = yes, N = no, C = conditional, P = partial.
- **Flag columns:** Vegan | GF (glutenFree) | LF (lactoseFree) | Diab (diabeticFriendly) | OGF (onionGarlicFree) | Fast (ekadashiSafe / navratriSafe / jainSafe).
- **Fasting note:** For all 111 dishes ekadashiSafe = navratriSafe = jainSafe = **No** (grain/maida/rice-based, and/or onion-garlic and/or root vegetables). Shown as `N/N/N`. Do not offer these on fast days.
- **"Conditional" (C)** means a simple documented swap achieves the constraint (e.g., tofu for paneer → vegan). **"Partial" (P)** for diabeticFriendly means acceptable only in small portions or with a lower-GI swap (brown rice, whole-wheat pasta).

