## Recommendations
1. **Import in weighted phases.** Load Indo-Chinese (22) and Italian (15) first — they are the highest-usage cuisines and will drive engagement — then Continental/American (20), then Mexican/Thai/Middle Eastern, then East Asian, desserts and beverages.
2. **Lead with vegetarian defaults.** For every paneer/tofu-swappable dish, default the veg version and expose the non-veg as a variant, given the large vegetarian user base.
3. **Surface the always-on health flags prominently** (vegan, glutenFree, lactoseFree, diabeticFriendly, onionGarlicFree) and treat fasting flags as day-gated (auto-hidden on Ekadashi/Navratri/fast days). Benchmark: if a user sets "diabetic" as a household constraint, the app should filter to only Y/P items and warn on P.
4. **Encode the substitution notes as structured tips** (tofu↔paneer, tamari↔soy, soy↔fish sauce, plant-milk↔dairy, rajma↔black beans, hung curd↔sour cream) so the AI step-generator can auto-adapt recipes to a household's constraints.
5. **Re-weight annually** against the latest Swiggy/Zomato year-end reports. Trigger to expand a cuisine: if Korean or Middle Eastern sustains >40% YoY order growth (Korean was already +50% YoY on Swiggy in 2025), promote them from "emerging" (currently 10 and 9 dishes) to a full block of 15–20.

## Caveats
- Popularity figures come from Swiggy's and Zomato's annual year-end marketing reports (self-reported, unaudited platform data) and GlobalData menu analysis. Swiggy's own 2024 release even lists its chicken-roll and burger figures inconsistently across paragraphs, so treat order counts as directional, not precise.
- Compliance flags are conservative defaults for typical home-cook recipes; exact values shift with the specific recipe (e.g., adding egg, using tamari, swapping tofu). The app should let users override per household.
- The diabeticFriendly flag reflects glycemic load, not calories: white rice (GI up to ~93), refined-flour bases and deep-fried/sugary items are marked No/Partial; lean-protein and legume/vegetable-forward dishes (grilled chicken steak, hummus, baba ganoush, refried beans, shakshuka, Caprese) are the genuine Yes cases. "Partial" means acceptable only in controlled portions or with a lower-GI swap (brown rice, whole-wheat pasta).
- Jain classification is honest and strict: nearly all dishes use onion/garlic and/or root vegetables and are marked jainSafe = No; a few (Caprese, plain jasmine rice) are onion-garlic-free but still not fasting-compliant in the app's model.
- This is a metadata dataset only — per the brief, no step-by-step cooking instructions are included, since the app generates those on demand.